    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bf659307_52f21f08",
        "filename": "keystone/identity/backends/sql.py",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 265,
      "author": {
        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-04-09T17:56:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Doesn\u0027t this kind of defeat the purpose of an override? I wonder if we should be more prescriptive of how this should be used. Without diving in to the implementation details, I could see this playing out a few different ways;\n\n1.) An operator sets CONF.security_compliance.minimum_password_age \u003d -1, meaning no one can change their password. Should they be able to override the global configuration option for specific users (e.g. service users?). I don\u0027t think this would allow that.\n\n2.) An operator sets CONF.security_compliance.minimum_password_age to some value, and then going through and updates each applicable user to have a value of -1 is they shouldn\u0027t be able to update their password. This would work with the above logic.\n\nIs #2 what we\u0027re shooting for and I just misinterpreted it? If #2 is the design or flow we want, we could implement this without overloading the minimum_password_age option and just have a user specific option for ``allow_password_update`` and make it a boolean.\n\nJust thinking about the usability of this and wondering if we actually need to supply multiple ways to do this, or if we can be more prescriptive.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 264,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 265,
        "endChar": 60
      },
      "revId": "e21d907045efa800effdc2ee124eaf824ee2b1bd",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bf659307_3ed094c9",
        "filename": "keystone/identity/backends/sql.py",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 265,
      "author": {
        "id": 2903
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-04-09T20:35:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I strongly believe we should support overrides for individual users, however, as the global config is set by the operator of the cloud [users may be adjusted by a domain admin], the operator should set global policy and we should not allow overrides that are less strict than the global.\n\nThe workflow is mostly #2, however, I was aiming to allow for individual users to also have a longer min-password requirement. Long term, we should also allow User-ROs to be defaulted at the domain level (all users in domain X have options set [e.g. MFA, etc]).\n\nI could drop the global -1 effect, but it felt like the best way to allow an entire installation to lean on external password management.\n\nThe design was a mix of concerns I was trying to address and avoid a ton of extra options [config/user]. I\u0027m happy to split to an explicit user option fi that is the direction you\u0027d rather go.",
      "parentUuid": "bf659307_52f21f08",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 264,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 265,
        "endChar": 60
      },
      "revId": "e21d907045efa800effdc2ee124eaf824ee2b1bd",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bf659307_dce161fe",
        "filename": "keystone/identity/backends/sql.py",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 265,
      "author": {
        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-04-09T20:58:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What do you think about splitting the global toggle from the user options toggle? It might make the change simpler and still fill the requirement that users have for external systems. If the request comes back in the future, we can evaluate extending the logic to support more complicated override cases\n\n\u003cshrug/\u003e",
      "parentUuid": "bf659307_3ed094c9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 264,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 265,
        "endChar": 60
      },
